



{"id":"da42d9c9-90bc-4cab-9a2b-0bdc4bf71cd0","object":"chat.completion","created":1786448330,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_060\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_05\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 22,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 5,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_08\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 24,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 14,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 5,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_11\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 26,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_07\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_03\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 20,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV integrated repair. The instruction says: \"Return only one JSON object that exactly follows the requested schema.\" The schema is: {\"record_id\":\"...\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"...\",\"uav_id\":\"...\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"...\",\"depart_slot\":0,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}. We need to produce one complete repair covering every mission exactly once, respecting fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nWe are given a baseline set of decisions (which might not be valid after perturbations). We need to produce a new set of decisions. The baseline is:\n\n- MISSION_01: UAV_02, PATH_05, depart_slot 22, held false, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_02: UAV_03, PATH_01, depart_slot 5, held false, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_03: UAV_03, PATH_08, depart_slot 24, held false, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_04: UAV_01, PATH_04, depart_slot 14, held false, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_05: UAV_01, PATH_07, depart_slot 5, held false, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_06: UAV_02, PATH_11, depart_slot 26, held false, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_07: UAV_03, PATH_10, depart_slot 8, held false, charge_blocks 0\n\nThe interventions include: closed COR_02 slots from 8 to 47? Actually list shows slot 8 to slot 47? It lists \"slot\":8 up to \"slot\":47, but horizon_slots is 45, so may be up to 45? Also closed COR_07 slots from 8 to 19. Also COR_01 capacity override to 1 at slot 5. Initial SOC overrides: for UAV_01: initial_soc_kwh: 2.885956 (originally was 9.479298). Other UAVs initial SOC unchanged: UAV_02: 8.5, UAV_03: 3.324565.\n\nWe must respect fixed UAV assignment and fixed UAV order: that means each UAV's missions must be served in the order given by the sequence_index. So UAV_01 has missions: MISSION_05 (seq 0, release 5, due 11), MISSION_04 (seq 1, release 14, due 23). That order is fixed: 05 then 04. UAV_02 has: MISSION_01 (seq 0, release 22, due 29), MISSION_06 (seq 1, release 26, due 32). So order: 01 then 06. UAV_03 has: MISSION_02 (seq 0, release 5, due 7), MISSION_07 (seq 1, release 8, due 25), MISSION_03 (seq 2, release 24, due 28). So order: 02, 07, 03. That is the fixed order.\n\nWe need to assign a path, depart_slot, and charge_blocks (0 to max_charge_blocks=3) for each mission, or hold it (held true). Urgent holds? Not defined, but likely attempting to minimize holds, especially urgent (maybe some missions are priority routine, no \"urgent\" mentioned, so all routine). So maybe no holds if possible.\n\nWe need to compute feasibility: For each mission, the UAV must be at the origin vertiport at the depart_slot (starting from its initial vertiport and initial SOC after previous missions and charging). The mission consumes energy for incoming deadhead (if any), service flight, reserve check. The UAV must not exceed battery capacity after charging. There is turnaround after landing: destination turnaround = 1 slot? The \"turnaround_slots\" is 1 in the instance. So after landing (arrival = depart_slot + travel_slots), there is a turnaround of 1 slot, then the UAV can begin charging. The charge_blocks attached to a mission occur at that mission's destination after its service flight and the stated turnaround. So charge_blocks number of 0.95 kWh (UAV_01, UAV_02) or 1.2 kWh (UAV_03) per block, each block takes 1 slot? Typically, charging takes 1 slot per block? The description: \"charge_kwh_per_block\":0.95, and we can choose charge_blocks up to max_charge_blocks=3. So if we set charge_blocks = k, then the UAV charges for k slots after turnaround, gaining k*charge_kwh_per_block energy, and then becomes available for next mission. During charging, the UAV occupies a charger at the destination vertiport for those slots.\n\nWe must also respect charger capacity (default 2 per vertiport) and corridor capacity (default 2, but some overrides). Also closed corridors: COR_02 is closed from slot 8 through 45 (maybe beyond 45, but horizon is 45, so closed for all relevant slots). COR_07 is closed from slot 8 to 19. Corridor COR_01 capacity=1 at slot 5.\n\nAdditionally, we have deadhead: All missions have incoming_deadhead=null, meaning there is no pre-registered incoming deadhead path; the UAV is assumed to be already at the origin? Wait: \"incoming deadhead is null\" for all missions. But the baseline had deadhead_contract: \"FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH\". However all missions have incoming_deadhead:null, which likely means no deadhead needed (the UAV is already positioned). But wait: The normative SOC event order says: \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". So if no incoming deadhead, that energy is zero. But we still need to ensure UAV is at the origin at the start of the mission. The UAV's state is determined by previous missions and charging. For the first mission of each UAV, the UAV starts at its start_vertiport (VP_02 for all). So UAV_01 starts at VP_02 with SOC 2.885956. Its first mission MISSION_05 origin is VP_02, so no deadhead needed; it's already there. MISSION_04 origin is VP_06, so after MISSION_05, UAV_01 will be at VP_06 (destination of MISSION_05). Then for MISSION_04, it needs to be at VP_06, so again no deadhead. So indeed no deadhead is needed because origins match the previous destinations (or start point). For UAV_02: starts at VP_02, MISSION_01 origin VP_02, so no deadhead. Then MISSION_06 origin VP_05, and MISSION_01 destination is VP_05, so no deadhead. For UAV_03: starts at VP_02, MISSION_02 origin VP_02, no deadhead; MISSION_07 origin VP_06 (MISSION_02 destination VP_06), so no deadhead; MISSION_03 origin VP_07 (MISSION_07 destination VP_07), so no deadhead. So deadhead energy is 0 for all missions.\n\nThus the problem reduces to scheduling these flights and charging events, respecting battery constraints, turnarounds, and resource capacities.\n\nNow, we must choose paths, depart slots, charge_blocks for each mission. Baseline gave a solution, but we need to repair due to overrides: initial SOC of UAV_01 reduced to 2.885956 (from 9.479298). Also corridor closures and capacity reductions. The baseline might not be feasible. We must produce a new feasible solution that optimizes lexicographically: urgent holds (none likely, so 0 holds), total holds (minimize), repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. \"repair distance\" probably means sum of differences in some metric? Possibly it's the edit distance from baseline. Since we are told to return one complete repair, and the baseline is given as a candidate, we can try to mimic it as closely as possible unless forced to change by constraints.\n\nWe should first evaluate feasibility of baseline under new initial SOC and closures.\n\nLet's list missions with baseline decisions and check.\n\nWe'll need to compute energy usage and check reserve.\n\nUAV_01: initial SOC = 2.885956 kWh. Battery capacity = 22.0, reserve = 2.5. So usable energy to start before reserve = initial SOC - reserve = 0.385956 kWh. That's very low. MISSION_05: baseline path PATH_07, travel_slots 7, energy_kwh: 5.853666 (including payload and wind?). Actually path energy given: PATH_07 energy_kwh = 5.853666. That's the total flight energy? It says energy_kwh: 5.853666. That likely already accounts for payload, wind, etc. So need 5.853666 kWh. With initial 2.885956, after flight, SOC would be 2.885956 - 5.853666 = -2.96771, below zero. Not feasible. So baseline for MISSION_05 is infeasible. We need to change path or hold or charge before flight? But the event order: For the first mission, there is no pre-charge other than initial SOC. The UAV cannot charge before its first flight because it starts at VP_02 and immediately begins mission at release slot. There's no opportunity to charge before departure because charging only happens after missions. So MISSION_05 must be feasible with initial SOC. With only 2.885956 kWh, the maximum energy it can expend is 2.885956 - 2.5 = 0.385956 kWh. So we need a path with energy <= 0.385956 kWh. Available paths for MISSION_05 (UAV_01, from VP_02 to VP_06): PATH_02 (energy 0.3322), PATH_07 (5.853666), PATH_09 (0.31408). So PATH_02 (energy 0.3322) <= 0.385956, feasible. PATH_09 (0.31408) also feasible. PATH_07 too high. So we must switch to one of the low-energy paths. That will change travel time (slots). PATH_02 has travel_slots 3, PATH_09 travel_slots 2. Also need to consider corridor availability. For PATH_02, corridor COR_06 from VP_02 to VP_06, duration 3, offset 0, wind_level 0. Need to check if COR_06 has capacity and not closed. COR_06 not listed in closures, capacity default 2. PATH_09 uses COR_01, duration 2, offset 0, wind_level 0. But COR_01 has capacity override: slot 5 capacity 1. So at slot 5, there is already UAV_03's MISSION_02 using COR_01? In baseline, MISSION_02 by UAV_03 departs slot 5, path PATH_01 which uses COR_01 (from VP_02 to VP_06) with travel_slots 2. So COR_01 at slot 5 is used by UAV_03. Capacity at slot 5 is 1, so only one UAV can use it. If we assign PATH_09 to MISSION_05 departing slot 5, then two UAVs would use COR_01 at slot 5, exceeding capacity. So PATH_09 is not feasible at slot 5 if MISSION_02 also uses COR_01 at slot 5. Could we delay MISSION_05? Release slot is 5, so can depart >=5. If we depart at slot 6, then COR_01 would be used during slot 6 (travel_slots 2, so slots 6-7? Actually travel_slots: 2, so if depart 6, arrival 8? Need to check slot occupancy: edge segment duration_slots:2, offset 0. So the corridor is occupied for departure slot and maybe next? Typically, a mission using a corridor occupies it for the duration_slots starting from the depart_slot? Possibly the departure slot is the time the UAV enters the corridor, and it occupies it for duration_slots. So if depart 6, it uses corridor slot 6 and 7? We need to respect corridor closures: COR_02 closed 8-..., COR_07 closed 8-19. Should also check corridor capacity for each slot. So MISSION_05 could depart at slot 5 but with PATH_02 (COR_06) to avoid COR_01 conflict. PATH_02 energy 0.3322, feasible. PATH_09 would conflict. So we can choose PATH_02 for MISSION_05. Then travel_slots 3, so arrival = depart+3. Baseline depart 5 -> arrival 8. Then turnaround 1 slot (slot 9), then can charge. That works.\n\nBut we also have MISSION_04 for UAV_01 after MISSION_05, from VP_06 to VP_04. Baseline path PATH_04 (energy 0.125632, travel_slots 1). So that's fine. The UAV's SOC after MISSION_05: initial 2.885956 - 0.3322 = 2.553756. After reserve check, must have at least 2.5 reserve? The reserve check is done after service flight energy is deducted. So SOC after flight must be >= reserve? The condition: \"Check reserve immediately after incoming-deadhead plus service energy are deducted.\" So if SOC after flight < reserve_kwh (2.5), it's invalid. Here, 2.553756 > 2.5, okay. Then we can charge. At VP_06, turnaround slot 9, then post-service charge: we can choose charge_blocks. Later, for MISSION_04, we need SOC to be at least (energy of MISSION_04 + reserve). MISSION_04 energy 0.125632, so required pre-flight SOC >= 2.5+0.125632 = 2.625632. After MISSION_05 (before charging) SOC is 2.553756. So we need at least 2.625632 - 2.553756 = 0.071876 kWh additional. So 1 charge block of 0.95 kWh would bring SOC to 2.553756+0.95=3.503756, enough. So we can set charge_blocks=1 for MISSION_05. But we also need to consider timing: MISSION_04 release slot is 14. MISSION_05 arrival slot: if depart 5, travel 3, arrival 8, turnaround slot 9, then 1 charge block occupies slot 10? Charging occurs after turnaround, and each block likely takes 1 slot. So charging slot 10. Then UAV available at slot 11. Depart for MISSION_04 must be >= release 14 and after previous mission end. Slot 11 is before 14, so we can depart at 14. That works. However, we need to check charger capacity at VP_06 during slot 10. No overrides, capacity 2. At that time, other UAVs might be charging there. We'll need to ensure occupancy.\n\nNow, baseline for MISSION_04 depart_slot 14, path PATH_04 (travel 1), arrival 15, turnaround 16, charge_blocks 0. That's fine.\n\nBut we also need to consider the objective: repair distance, weighted tardiness, etc. We want to minimize hold (preferably 0). So we should try to keep all missions unheld.\n\nNow, check UAV_02: initial SOC 8.5, MISSION_01 baseline path PATH_05 (energy 0.160815, travel 2), depart 22, arrival 24, turnaround 25, charge_blocks 0. SOC after flight: 8.5 - 0.160815 = 8.339185, >2.5 reserve. Then MISSION_06: origin VP_05, path PATH_11 (energy 0.125436, travel 1), depart baseline 26. But arrival of MISSION_01 is 24, turnaround 25, so available at 26. So depart 26 works. SOC before MISSION_06: 8.339185 (no charging, charge_blocks=0). That minus 0.125436 = 8.213749 >2.5. So feasible. But any corridor closures? PATH_05 uses COR_05, not closed. PATH_11 uses COR_03, not closed. Capacities fine.\n\nUAV_03: initial SOC 3.324565, reserve 1.5, battery cap 16. Missions: MISSION_02 baseline: PATH_01 (energy 0.23504, travel 2), depart 5, arrival 7, turnaround 8. Then MISSION_07 baseline: PATH_10 (energy 0.130447, travel 2), depart 8? Arrival of MISSION_02 is 7, turnaround 8, so available at 9. But baseline depart_slot for MISSION_07 is 8. That's impossible because it would conflict with turnaround? Turnaround is the time after landing before charging or next mission: \"destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". The UAV must complete turnaround before it can be used for next flight. Turnaround takes 1 slot. So if it lands at slot 7, turnaround occupies slot 8, so it cannot depart at slot 8. So baseline is infeasible already due to turnaround. MISSION_07 must depart >=9. Release slot is 8, due 25. So we need to adjust. Also, MISSION_07 path PATH_10 uses COR_02? No, PATH_10 uses COR_02? Let's check: PATH_10 has corridor_ids: [\"COR_02\"]? Actually from MISSION_07 path_options: PATH_03 uses COR_07, PATH_06 uses COR_11, PATH_10 uses COR_02. Wait: given: PATH_10: corridor_ids: [\"COR_02\"], edge_segments: [{\"corridor_id\":\"COR_02\",\"duration_slots\":2,\"offset_slot\":0,\"wind_level\":1}], so it uses COR_02. But COR_02 is closed from slot 8 onward! So PATH_10 is not available at any slot >=8 because COR_02 closed starting slot 8. So we must change path for MISSION_07. The other options: PATH_03 uses COR_07 (closed slots 8-19, so if depart slot... travel_slots 2, if depart at 9, then uses corridor slots 9 and 10, which are closed (8-19). So not allowed. PATH_06 uses COR_11, not closed, energy 0.94016, travel_slots 14. That's long but feasible if timing and energy allow. Energy 0.94016, travel 14. So we can use PATH_06, depart at some slot after MISSION_02 turnaround (available at 9), release 8, due 25. Departure must be >= max(9, 8) = 9. Travel 14, arrival = depart+14, so if depart 9, arrival 23, turnaround 24, then MISSION_03 baseline depart 24, path PATH_08 (travel 1) from VP_07 to VP_01. But MISSION_03 release 24, due 28. So if MISSION_07 arrives 23, turnaround 24, then MISSION_03 could depart at 24, but need to check turnaround: MISSION_07 arrival 23, turnaround 24, so available at 24. That's okay. But we need to check energy: initial SOC for UAV_03: 3.324565. MISSION_02 energy 0.23504, after flight SOC = 3.089525. Reserve check: 3.089525 > 1.5, ok. Then we can charge after MISSION_02 at VP_06. Turnaround for MISSION_02 is slot 8? MISSION_02 dep 5, travel 2, arr 7, turnaround slot 8. So post-service charge can start slot 9? Actually turnaround occupies slot 8, so charging starts slot 9. We can set charge_blocks for MISSION_02. Then MISSION_07 energy 0.94016, so required pre-flight SOC >= 1.5+0.94016 = 2.44016. Current after MISSION_02: 3.089525, which is above 2.44016, so no charging needed. But we might still charge if we want to leave room for MISSION_03. MISSION_03 energy 1.043578, required pre-flight SOC >= 2.543578. After MISSION_07, SOC will be 3.089525 - 0.94016 = 2.149365. That is less than 2.543578, so not enough for MISSION_03. Thus we need to charge between MISSION_02 and MISSION_07 to increase SOC before MISSION_07, or charge after MISSION_07 before MISSION_03. MISSION_03 is immediately after MISSION_07 (depart 24 if same as baseline). So if we charge after MISSION_07, there must be time between arrival of MISSION_07 (23) and departure of MISSION_03 (24). Arrival 23, turnaround 24, so charging could not start until slot 25, which is after MISSION_03's release and due? MISSION_03 release 24, so it must depart at or after 24. If we charge after MISSION_07, the UAV would be busy charging during slot 25, so cannot depart at 24. So the only chance to charge before MISSION_03 is to charge after MISSION_02, before departing for MISSION_07. That would push MISSION_07 departure later. So we need to decide a schedule: MISSION_02 depart 5 (fixed by baseline? Could depart later? Release 5, due 7, so must depart such that arrival <=7? Arrival = depart + travel_slots. travel=2, so depart must be <=5 to arrive by 7? due_slot is 7, so arrival <=7? Typically due_slot means the mission must be completed (arrival) by that slot? The definition: \"due_slot\":7, so arrival slot <= due_slot? In baseline, depart 5, travel 2, arrival 7, so exactly due. So we can't delay MISSION_02 beyond 5. So MISSION_02 must depart at 5. So after MISSION_02, we can charge starting slot 8 (turnaround) or slot 9? Let's clarify: For MISSION_02: service flight from slot 5 to 7 (arrival 7). Then turnaround: destination turnaround takes 1 slot. So the UAV is in turnaround at slot 8? Or does turnaround occur immediately after landing, occupying the next slot? Typically, if arrival is slot 7, the UAV lands at the end of slot 7, then turnaround takes slot 8. After that, it is available for next mission or charging. So post-service charge can begin at slot 9? Or can it begin at slot 8 if turnaround and charge are separate? The normative order: service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So destination turnaround happens before post-service charge. So the first charge block occurs after turnaround. So if arrival slot is t, turnaround occupies slot t+1, and charge starts at t+2. For MISSION_02: arrival slot 7, turnaround slot 8, charge block 1 at slot 9. So the UAV would be available for next mission at slot 9 if no charge, or after charge. But MISSION_07 in baseline depart 8 would conflict with turnaround. So MISSION_07 must depart at earliest 9 if no charge, but if we add charge blocks, later. So we need to schedule MISSION_07 after charging.\n\nSo, we need to decide charge_blocks for MISSION_02. Then MISSION_07 will depart after those blocks, with path PATH_06 (14 slots) to arrive by due 25. If we add k charge blocks, MISSION_07 departs at: after MISSION_02 arrival 7, turnaround 8, so k charge blocks occupy slots 9...8+k (if k>=1). Actually, if k=0, available at slot 9 (since turnaround 8). So earliest depart for MISSION_07 = 9 if k=0. With PATH_06 travel 14, arrival = depart+14. So if depart 9, arrival 23, which is before due 25, OK. If we add charge blocks, depart is later, e.g., k=1, depart >=9+1=10, arr 24; k=2, depart >=11, arr 25 (due slot is 25, so arrival 25 is okay if due means arrival <=25? probably). Then MISSION_03: after MISSION_07 arrival and turnaround, can depart. MISSION_03 release 24, due 28, travel 1. So if MISSION_07 arrives 24 with k=2, turnaround 25, then MISSION_03 could depart 25, arrival 26, okay. Need energy: After MISSION_02, SOC = 3.089525. If we charge k blocks, each block gives 1.2 kWh, so SOC becomes 3.089525 + k*1.2. For k=0, SOC after MISSION_02 is 3.089525. Then MISSION_07 consumes 0.94016, leaving 2.149365. Then MISSION_03 needs 1.043578 + 1.5 reserve = 2.543578, not enough. For k=1, SOC after charge (before MISSION_07) = 4.289525. Then after MISSION_07: 4.289525 - 0.94016 = 3.349365, which is >2.543578, so enough for MISSION_03. So k=1 is sufficient. k=1 means one charge block of 1.2 kWh, taking 1 slot: slot 9. So MISSION_07 can depart at 10 (after slot 9 charge). Depart 10, travel 14, arrival 24, turnaround 25, then MISSION_03 depart 25, arrival 26, within due 28. That works. Need to check charger capacity at VP_06 during slot 9: only one charger used by UAV_03? Also UAV_01 might be charging at VP_06 later. At slot 9, what other UAVs? UAV_01 after MISSION_05 arrival 8, turnaround 9? Let's check UAV_01 schedule with new path.\n\nWe need to design a complete schedule for all UAVs, respecting all resources, and then compare to baseline for repair distance. The objective order: urgent holds (0), total holds (0 hopefully), repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nWe can try to produce a solution that is as close to baseline as possible, but with necessary changes. Baseline for UAV_01 had MISSION_05 path PATH_07, depart 5. We change to PATH_02, depart 5 maybe, keeping depart_slot 5. For MISSION_04, keep PATH_04, depart 14, charge_blocks 0. So that matches baseline depart slots and holds (0). For UAV_02, baseline is already feasible, so we can keep MISSION_01 PATH_05 depart 22, charge_blocks 0; MISSION_06 PATH_11 depart 26, charge_blocks 0. For UAV_03, baseline MISSION_02 PATH_01 depart 5, charge_blocks 0 (but we need to charge 1 block, so charge_blocks=1). MISSION_07 PATH_10 depart 8 is infeasible, so we change to PATH_06, depart 10 (since charge_blocks=1). Then MISSION_03 PATH_08 depart 24 baseline, but after MISSION_07 arrival 24, we need to adjust depart to 25. So depart 25. That gives a solution with all missions unheld, but with some changes: UAV_01 path change, UAV_03 path and depart changes for MISSION_07 and MISSION_03, and charge_blocks change.\n\nNow we must verify constraints: corridor capacities and closures, charger capacities, pad capacities.\n\nFirst, list all flights with their corridor usage slots.\n\nWe'll assume that a flight using a corridor occupies the corridor during its travel_slots, starting from the depart_slot (maybe the UAV enters at depart slot and uses the corridor for the duration). For edge_segments, duration_slots is given, and the corridor capacity is per slot. So a mission with travel_slots = 2 would occupy the corridor for 2 consecutive slots beginning at depart_slot (or offset?). The edge_segment has offset_slot=0, so depart_slot = entry slot. So if depart_slot = 5, it occupies slot 5 and 6. But need to consider that travel_slots might include both entry and exit? In many models, the flight occupies the corridor for the entire duration, so for 2 slots, it uses slots t and t+1. We'll adopt that.\n\nAlso, pad capacity: each vertiport has pad capacity 2. When a UAV lands or takes off, it uses a pad. But the problem description doesn't detail pad occupancy. Typically, a UAV occupies a pad during turnaround and maybe charging? Possibly the pad is occupied during the entire ground time (turnaround + charge). We need to respect pad capacity. The normative order mentions \"destination turnaround\" and \"post-service charge\". Both happen at the destination vertiport. So from the arrival slot through turnaround and charging, the UAV is on a pad. Also, at the origin, the UAV occupies a pad during pre-flight? Possibly at departure, it uses a pad for a slot? The task doesn't specify origin pad occupancy; probably only destination ground time matters. But to be safe, we should ensure that at any vertiport, the number of UAVs simultaneously in turnaround or charging does not exceed pad capacity (default 2). Additionally, charger capacity limits the number of UAVs that can be charging concurrently. So if a UAV is charging, it uses both a pad and a charger. If it is only in turnaround (no charging), it still uses a pad.\n\nSo we need to schedule ground occupancies. For each mission, after arrival, from arrival_slot? Actually, turnaround starts immediately after landing, so maybe the UAV is on the pad from arrival_slot+1? Or from arrival_slot? Typically, a UAV lands at the end of a slot, so it occupies the pad during the next slot for turnaround. Many definitions: if a mission arrives at slot t, it occupies the destination vertiport from t+1 to t+1+turnaround+charge_blocks-1? Let's define clearly:\n\nSuppose a mission departs at slot d, travel duration s slots. The arrival slot is d+s? If it departs at slot d, it is in the air during slots d through d+s-1, and arrives at the end of slot d+s-1? Or the convention might be that a travel_slots of 2 means the UAV is in transit for 2 full slots, arriving at the start of the next slot. For instance, if depart slot 5, travel_slots 2, it might occupy corridor during slots 5 and 6, and land at the end of slot 6, so arrival = 6. In baseline, MISSION_02: depart 5, travel 2, due 7; due likely means arrival must be <=7, so arrival = 7? If travel 2 and depart 5, the arrival might be slot 7 (depart+travel). That suggests travel_slots is added to depart_slot to get arrival slot. So if depart 5, travel 2, arrival = 7. That means it is in transit during slots 5 and 6, and arrives at slot 7? That seems off because slot 7 would be the arrival slot, but the flight would take two slots (5 and 6) then arrive at some point in slot 7. Better to assume: departure at beginning of slot d, flight takes exactly s slots, arrival at end of slot d+s-1. Usually, the arrival slot is given as d+s-1? Let's check baseline: MISSION_02 depart 5, travel 2, due 7. If arrival is 5+2-1=6, then due 7 is fine. If arrival is 7, due 7 is exactly due. The baseline also for MISSION_01: depart 22, travel 2, arrival 24? 22+2=24, baseline MISSION_06 depart 26, so there is a gap? If arrival is 24, turnaround is 25, available 26, matches. For MISSION_05 (old path PATH_07 travel 7), depart 5, arrival = 12. Then MISSION_04 depart 14, so turnaround 13, available 14. So consistent with arrival = depart + travel_slots. So we will adopt: arrival_slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. Thus the UAV is in the air during slots [depart_slot, depart_slot + travel_slots - 1] (inclusive). It lands at the end of slot arrival_slot. Then turnaround takes the next slot: turnaround_slot = arrival_slot + 1. Post-service charge blocks start after turnaround: charge_slot_start = arrival_slot + 2, and last for charge_blocks slots: slots [arrival_slot+2, arrival_slot+1+charge_blocks]. If charge_blocks=0, no charging, UAV becomes available at the start of slot arrival_slot+2? Or is it available after turnaround at slot arrival_slot+1? The normative order: after turnaround, it either charges or is free. So if no charging, it becomes available at the start of the slot after turnaround, i.e., arrival_slot+2? But then it would be idle for one slot after turnaround? Actually, a turnaround slot means the UAV is busy during that slot, so it cannot be used for another mission until the slot after turnaround. So earliest next departure is arrival_slot + 2 (if no charge). In baseline MISSION_02: depart 5, travel 2, arrival 7. Then MISSION_07 baseline depart 8, which is arrival+1, which would conflict with turnaround if turnaround occupies slot 8. So indeed baseline is invalid unless turnaround is slot 8 and depart is at start of slot 8 (before turnaround?) Unlikely. So baseline is broken. So consistent: turnaround slot = arrival_slot+1, and UAV is busy that slot; next departure possible at arrival_slot+2.\n\nThus, for MISSION_02 with arrival 7, turnaround 8, available at 9. So MISSION_07 can depart earliest 9. With charge blocks k, charge occupies slots 9..8+k, so available at 9+k.\n\nNow, for pad/charger occupancy: The UAV occupies a pad at the destination vertiport during turnaround and charging. So from arrival_slot+1 to arrival_slot+1+turnaround_slots (1) + charge_blocks? The turnaround occupies 1 slot (arrival_slot+1). Charging occupies from arrival_slot+2 for charge_blocks slots. So the total ground occupancy at destination is from arrival_slot+1 to arrival_slot+1 + 1 + charge_blocks? If charge_blocks=0, it occupies only arrival_slot+1 for turnaround. But if charge_blocks>0, it occupies arrival_slot+1 (turnaround) and arrival_slot+2...arrival_slot+1+charge_blocks (charging). So total slots at destination: 1 (turnaround) + charge_blocks. For example, k=1: occupancy slots: arrival+1 (turnaround) and arrival+2 (charge). So if arrival = 7, occupancy slots 8 and 9. That matches: turnaround 8, charging 9, available at 10.\n\nNow, we need to check pad capacity: max 2 UAVs can be at a vertiport simultaneously (either in turnaround or charging). So we need to check for each slot each vertiport, the number of UAVs that are on the ground there does not exceed pad capacity (2). Similarly, charger capacity: the number of UAVs that are charging (i.e., in a charge block slot) does not exceed charger_count (2). For chargers, only charging slots count.\n\nAlso, we must ensure that at the start of a UAV's mission, it is at the origin vertiport. Since we have no deadhead, the UAV is already there from previous mission's destination or start. That is satisfied if the previous mission's destination equals the next mission's origin, which holds.\n\nNow, let's design the schedule with the changes we proposed and verify all capacities.\n\nUAV_01:\n- MISSION_05: path PATH_02 (COR_06, travel=3, energy=0.3322), depart_slot 5 (release 5, due 11). Arrival = 5+3=8. Turnaround slot 9 (occupies VP_06 pad). We set charge_blocks=1 (to enable MISSION_04). So charging slot 10 (occupies charger at VP_06). So ground occupancy at VP_06: slots 9 (turnaround) and 10 (charging). Available for next mission at start of slot 11.\n- MISSION_04: origin VP_06, path PATH_04 (COR_04, travel=1, energy=0.125632). Departure must be >= release 14 and >= available 11. We choose depart_slot 14 (baseline). Arrival = 15. Turnaround slot 16 at VP_04. charge_blocks=0. Ground occupancy at VP_04: slot 16. No charging.\n\nEnergy: Initial SOC 2.885956. After MISSION_05 flight: 2.885956 - 0.3322 = 2.553756 ( >2.5). After charging 1 block: +0.95 = 3.503756. Battery cap 22, so within. Before MISSION_04: SOC 3.503756. After MISSION_04: 3.503756 - 0.125632 = 3.378124. Reserve check: 3.378124 >2.5. OK.\n\nNow UAV_02:\n- MISSION_01: path PATH_05 (COR_05, travel=2, energy=0.160815), depart 22, arrival 24. Turnaround 25 at VP_05, charge_blocks=0. Occupancy: VP_05 pad slot 25. Available at 26.\n- MISSION_06: path PATH_11 (COR_03, travel=1, energy=0.125436), depart 26 (>=26, release 26), arrival 27. Turnaround 28 at VP_03, charge_blocks=0. Occupancy: VP_03 pad slot 28.\n\nEnergy: Initial 8.5. After MISSION_01: 8.339185. After MISSION_06: 8.213749. All >2.5. Battery ok.\n\nUAV_03:\n- MISSION_02: path PATH_01 (COR_01, travel=2, energy=0.23504), depart 5, arrival 7. Turnaround 8 at VP_06. We set charge_blocks=1. So charging slot 9 at VP_06. Occupancy: VP_06 pad slots 8 (turnaround) and 9 (charging). Available at 10.\n- MISSION_07: path PATH_06 (COR_11, travel=14, energy=0.94016), depart 10 (available 10, release 8), arrival 10+14=24. Turnaround 25 at VP_07. charge_blocks=0. Occupancy: VP_07 pad slot 25.\n- MISSION_03: path PATH_08 (COR_10, travel=1, energy=1.043578), depart must be after MISSION_07 turnaround (available at 26? Actually MISSION_07 arrival 24, turnaround 25, so available at 26. Release 24, due 28. So depart 26. Arrival 27. Turnaround 28 at VP_01, charge_blocks=0. Occupancy: VP_01 pad slot 28.\n\nEnergy for UAV_03: Initial SOC 3.324565. MISSION_02: after flight: 3.089525. Reserve ok. Charge 1 block: +1.2 = 4.289525. MISSION_07: after flight: 4.289525 - 0.94016 = 3.349365. Reserve check: >1.5. MISSION_03: after flight: 3.349365 - 1.043578 = 2.305787, >1.5. Battery cap 16, all within.\n\nNow check corridor capacities and closures:\n\n- COR_01: used by MISSION_02 from depart 5, travel 2, so occupies slots 5 and 6. Capacity override: at slot 5, capacity=1. Slot 5: only one use (MISSION_02). No conflict. Slot 6: default capacity 2, one use. OK.\n- COR_06: used by MISSION_05 PATH_02, travel 3, depart 5, occupies slots 5,6,7. Not closed, capacity default 2. OK.\n- COR_04: MISSION_04 PATH_04, travel 1, depart 14, occupies slot 14. OK.\n- COR_05: MISSION_01 PATH_05, depart 22, travel 2, slots 22,23. OK.\n- COR_03: MISSION_06 PATH_11, depart 26, travel 1, slot 26. OK.\n- COR_11: MISSION_07 PATH_06, depart 10, travel 14, occupies slots 10-23. Not closed. COR_11 not listed in closures, capacity 2. OK.\n- COR_10: MISSION_03 PATH_08, depart 26, travel 1, slot 26. OK.\n\nCheck closures: COR_02 closed from slot 8, COR_07 closed 8-19. Neither used.\n\nNow check charger capacity: VP_06 has charger capacity 2. At slot 9, UAV_03 is charging (1 block). At slot 10, UAV_01 is charging (1 block). No overlap. So charger count 1 each slot. OK. Other vertiports: no charging, so chargers not used.\n\nPad capacity: VP_06: we have turnaround/charging occupancies:\n- MISSION_02: turnaround slot 8, charge slot 9 -> UAV_03 occupies VP_06 pad during slots 8 and 9.\n- MISSION_05: turnaround slot 9, charge slot 10 -> UAV_01 occupies VP_06 pad during slots 9 and 10.\nSo at slot 9, both UAV_03 (charging) and UAV_01 (turnaround? Actually UAV_01 turnaround is slot 9, so they both occupy pad). That means two UAVs on pads at VP_06 at slot 9. Pad capacity is 2, so okay. At slot 8, only UAV_03. Slot 10, only UAV_01. So pad capacity satisfied.\n\nVP_05: MISSION_01 turnaround slot 25, pad occupied by UAV_02. OK.\nVP_03: MISSION_06 turnaround slot 28, UAV_02. OK.\nVP_07: MISSION_07 turnaround slot 25, UAV_03. OK.\nVP_04: MISSION_04 turnaround slot 16, UAV_01. OK.\nVP_01: MISSION_03 turnaround slot 28, UAV_03. OK.\n\nAll capacities fine.\n\nNow, check timing constraints: due slots and release slots. MISSION_05: release 5, depart 5, due 11, arrival 8, so completion by slot 8 <= due 11 (if due means arrival? possibly \"due_slot\" is the slot by which the mission must be completed, meaning arrival? In baseline, MISSION_02 due 7, arrival 7. So yes, arrival must be <= due_slot). So 8 <= 11 ok. MISSION_04: release 14, depart 14, arrival 15 <= due 23. OK. MISSION_02: release 5, depart 5, arrival 7 <= due 7. OK. MISSION_01: release 22, depart 22, arrival 24 <= due 29. OK. MISSION_06: release 26, depart 26, arrival 27 <= due 32. OK. MISSION_07: release 8, depart 10, arrival 24 <= due 25. OK. MISSION_03: release 24, depart 26, arrival 27 <= due 28. OK. So no holds needed.\n\nNow, compute the repair distance and other objectives. The baseline is given as a candidate solution. The repair distance likely measures how many decisions changed. We need to output a full set of decisions, so we'll output the ones we decided. The changes relative to baseline: MISSION_05 path changed (PATH_02 vs PATH_07), charge_blocks changed? baseline charge_blocks=0 for all, we have charge_blocks=1 for MISSION_05 and MISSION_02. So those change. MISSION_07 path changed (PATH_06 vs PATH_10), depart_slot changed (10 vs 8). MISSION_03 depart_slot changed (26 vs 24). MISSION_02 charge_blocks changed (1 vs 0). So repair distance might count these differences. But the optimization is lexicographic: urgent holds (0), total holds (0), then repair distance. So we want to minimize repair distance, meaning we want to change as few decisions as possible while still feasible. Could we have kept MISSION_05 charge_blocks=0? MISSION_04 energy requirement: SOC after MISSION_05 without charge is 2.553756, need 2.625632, so without charge, insufficient. So charge_blocks must be at least 1. Could we have moved MISSION_04 to a different path? Only PATH_04 is available, energy fixed. So charge necessary.\n\nCould we have used PATH_09 for MISSION_05? That would depart slot 5, travel 2, energy 0.31408, arrival 7, then SOC after flight = 2.885956-0.31408=2.571876 >2.5. That would leave more SOC, but still need for MISSION_04: after MISSION_05, SOC 2.571876, MISSION_04 needs 2.625632, still insufficient. So still need charge. Also PATH_09 conflicts with MISSION_02 at slot 5 COR_01 capacity. We could delay MISSION_05 departure to avoid conflict? But MISSION_05 release 5, so can depart >=5. If we depart later, e.g., slot 6, then arrival 8 (if travel 2). Then need charge. It would still need charging. So charge_blocks=1 is necessary. So MISSION_05 charge_blocks must be 1.\n\nFor MISSION_02, we could try to avoid charging by changing MISSION_07 and MISSION_03 paths? MISSION_03 energy 1.043578, MISSION_07 energy must be low enough that SOC after two flights is enough. Without charge, after MISSION_02, SOC=3.089525. MISSION_07 energy + MISSION_03 energy <= 3.089525 - 1.5 (reserve for last) = 1.589525. But MISSION_03 alone is 1.043578, so MISSION_07 must be <= 0.545947. The available paths for MISSION_07: PATH_03 energy 0.130447, PATH_10 energy 0.130447, PATH_06 energy 0.94016. So PATH_03 or PATH_10 energy 0.130447 would fit: total = 0.130447+1.043578=1.174025, less than 1.589525, so feasible without charge. But PATH_03 uses COR_07 which is closed from slot 8-19. If we could depart MISSION_07 such that it doesn't overlap closed slots, maybe depart at a slot that avoids closure? Closure is from slot 8, so any depart slot >=8 would mean COR_07 occupied from depart slot for 2 slots. If depart at 20, then slots 20-21, which are after closure (closed 8-19). So MISSION_07 could depart at 20, travel 2, arrival 22, using COR_07. PATH_10 uses COR_02 which is closed from 8 onward, so not possible at all. So if we want to avoid charging, we could use PATH_03 departing at 20 (after corridor reopens). But then MISSION_07 would arrive at 22, turnaround 23, then MISSION_03 depart >=24, arrival 25, still within due 28. However, MISSION_07 has due 25, and arrival 22 is fine. So that would be feasible and avoid charge. However, MISSION_07 would depart at 20, which is much later than baseline 8, and would significantly increase repair distance (depart_slot change from 8 to 20, path change from PATH_10 to PATH_03, and also MISSION_03 depart would be 24 still). But does it reduce total charge_blocks? Yes, we could set charge_blocks=0 for MISSION_02, and overall fewer charge_blocks. But the lexicographic order: after total holds, repair distance. We need to compare repair distance: the baseline has charge_blocks=0 for MISSION_02, depart_slot 8 for MISSION_07, path PATH_10. The alternative with charging has MISSION_02 charge_blocks=1 (changed), MISSION_07 depart_slot 10 (changed from 8, diff=2), path PATH_06 (changed). The other alternative without charging would have MISSION_02 charge_blocks=0 (same as baseline), MISSION_07 path PATH_03 (different), depart_slot 20 (diff=12). Which has smaller repair distance? Repair distance is not defined explicitly; it might be sum of absolute differences in depart_slot, + number of path changes + charge_blocks differences? Or maybe it's a syntactic edit distance. Usually in such tasks, repair distance measures the number of changes relative to the baseline, and we aim to minimize it. The baseline has 7 missions. Counting changes: For MISSION_02 charge_blocks from 0 to 1 -> 1 change. MISSION_07 path change (PATH_10 to PATH_06), depart_slot change (8 to 10) -> two changes (if path and slot are separate). MISSION_03 depart_slot from 24 to 26 -> change. Total changes: 4. In the no-charge alternative: MISSION_07 path change (PATH_10 to PATH_03), depart_slot change (8 to 20) -> two changes. MISSION_03 depart_slot may still be 24? In that scenario, after MISSION_07 arrival 22, turnaround 23, MISSION_03 can depart >=24, so depart 24, no change to MISSION_03 depart_slot. So changes: MISSION_07 path (1), MISSION_07 depart (1), possibly MISSION_03 unchanged. That's 2 changes. Also MISSION_02 charge_blocks unchanged. So that seems smaller repair distance (2 vs 4). But we also need to check if MISSION_05 charge_blocks change is still necessary; that's independent. So overall repair distance: with charge for MISSION_02: changes: MISSION_05 path (1), MISSION_05 charge (1), MISSION_02 charge (1), MISSION_07 path (1), MISSION_07 depart (1), MISSION_03 depart (1) = 6 changes. Without charge for MISSION_02: MISSION_05 path (1), MISSION_05 charge (1), MISSION_07 path (1), MISSION_07 depart (1) = 4 changes. So the no-charge alternative yields fewer deviations from baseline. So it might be preferred if feasible.\n\nBut we also have the objective \"delay plus charge wait, then energy\". Delay might refer to mission completion delay relative to release or something. Weighted tardiness might be based on due slots. With charging, MISSION_07 arrives at 24 (due 25, no tardiness), MISSION_03 arrives 27 (due 28, no tardiness). Without charging, MISSION_07 arrives 22 (due 25), MISSION_03 arrives 25 (due 28). No tardiness either. So tardiness not an issue. Delay plus charge wait maybe measures waiting time. So both might be fine.\n\nSo the no-charge alternative seems feasible and requires fewer changes. Let's verify all constraints for the no-charging scenario.\n\nUAV_03:\n- MISSION_02: PATH_01, depart 5, arrival 7, turnaround 8, charge_blocks=0. Available 9.\n- MISSION_07: PATH_03 (uses COR_07, travel 2, energy 0.130447). But COR_07 is closed from slot 8 to 19. So if we depart at 20, travel 2, using slots 20 and 21, which are not closed (closure up to 19, so 20 is open). So okay. Depart 20, arrival 22. Turnaround 23. charge_blocks=0. Occupancy: VP_07 pad slot 23.\n- MISSION_03: PATH_08, depart 24? Available after MISSION_07 turnaround at 23? Actually, MISSION_07 arrival 22, turnaround 23, so available 24. Depart 24, arrival 25. Turnaround 26 at VP_01. So depart 24 (same as baseline). So MISSION_03 unchanged.\n\nEnergy: initial 3.324565. After MISSION_02: 3.089525. After MISSION_07: 3.089525 - 0.130447 = 2.959078. After MISSION_03: 2.959078 - 1.043578 = 1.9155, above 1.5 reserve. OK.\n\nCorridors: COR_07 used slots 20-21. No other usage. COR_10 used slot 24. That's fine. No charger usage for UAV_03, so no charger occupancy at VP_06? MISSION_02 charge_blocks=0, so only turnaround at VP_06 slot 8, pad occupancy slot 8. Then UAV leaves VP_06? But MISSION_07 origin is VP_06; it needs to be at VP_06 at depart 20. The UAV after MISSION_02 is at VP_06 from arrival 7, turnaround 8, then available at 9. It remains at VP_06 until depart at 20. That is fine; it just waits there. No pad occupancy beyond turnaround? It is not occupying a pad while idle? Typically, a UAV sitting at a vertiport might occupy a pad. Pad capacity is 2; if it's just waiting, it may still need a pad. The problem doesn't specify if idle UAVs consume pad capacity. In many formulations, a UAV that has completed turnaround and is not charging is available but might still be on a pad. To be safe, we should consider that UAV occupies a pad from the time it arrives (or after turnaround) until it departs, or at least during turnaround and charging. The resource \"pad_capacity\" likely applies to active ground operations like turnaround and charging, and maybe also parking. The problem says \"pad_capacity_by_slot_default\" and pad capacity overrides, but no further description. In the baseline solution, UAV_03 after MISSION_02 turnaround at 8, then waits until depart at 8? Actually baseline had MISSION_07 depart at 8 immediately after arrival 7, which was invalid. So we can't infer. Usually, UAVs that are not actively being serviced might still occupy a pad. However, we should check if any pad capacity would be exceeded. At VP_06, we have UAV_01 after MISSION_05: arrival 8, turnaround 9, charge 10. So UAV_01 occupies pad at 9 and 10. UAV_03 occupies pad at 8 (turnaround). Then from 9 onward, UAV_03 is idle at VP_06. Does it continue to occupy a pad? If it does, then at slot 9, we have UAV_01 (turnaround) and UAV_03 (idle) both on pads, that's 2, ok. Slot 10: UAV_01 charging, UAV_03 idle, 2 pads. Slot 11-19: UAV_03 idle, uses 1 pad. At 20 it departs. So pad capacity 2 is never exceeded. So fine. For other vertiports, similar.\n\nNow, we need to also adjust MISSION_05: we already chose PATH_02 depart 5, charge_blocks=1. Could we avoid charging for MISSION_05 by using a path with lower energy for MISSI